1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,740
Hello everyone, and welcome back. We
are QAICON Lab, the Quantum and AI for

2
00:00:04,740 --> 00:00:08,968
Computing, Optimization, and
Networking Lab, at Western University.

3
00:00:09,367 --> 00:00:12,937
<font color="#7FD4E6">And today, we build quantum
codes out of classical ones.</font>

4
00:00:15,467 --> 00:00:19,424
At the end of the last section we
noticed that every check in Shor's

5
00:00:19,424 --> 00:00:23,440
code and in Steane's code is made
entirely of X's or entirely of Z's,

6
00:00:23,439 --> 00:00:27,868
and in Steane's code both halves come
from the same table of ones and zeros.

7
00:00:28,467 --> 00:00:31,810
That table is older than quantum
computing by decades, since it

8
00:00:31,810 --> 00:00:34,992
belongs to the Hamming code,
which engineers have used since

9
00:00:34,992 --> 00:00:38,443
the nineteen fifties to repair a
single flipped bit in a message.

10
00:00:39,033 --> 00:00:42,625
So here's a tempting recipe.
Take a classical code you like,

11
00:00:42,625 --> 00:00:46,278
write its table once with X's
and once with Z's, and call the

12
00:00:46,279 --> 00:00:49,506
result a quantum code. Do you
think that always works?

13
00:00:50,700 --> 00:00:54,587
<font color="#7FD4E6">Hmm, the Z checks catch bit
flips and the X checks catch</font>

14
00:00:54,587 --> 00:00:58,121
<font color="#7FD4E6">phase flips, so as long as
the code is good, right?</font>

15
00:00:58,467 --> 00:01:01,432
Well, that's half of the
answer, because a good code is

16
00:01:01,432 --> 00:01:04,451
certainly needed. But the half
that's missing is the one

17
00:01:04,451 --> 00:01:07,745
genuinely quantum part of the
story, so let's go and find it.

18
00:01:09,267 --> 00:01:12,271
So what does a table like this
actually do for a classical

19
00:01:12,271 --> 00:01:15,802
message? Each row picks out a few
positions, and it asks whether the

20
00:01:15,802 --> 00:01:18,753
bits sitting in those positions
add up to an even number.

21
00:01:19,333 --> 00:01:22,696
A message passes when every row
says even, and the messages

22
00:01:22,696 --> 00:01:26,175
that pass are the code, which
in linear algebra is called the

23
00:01:26,176 --> 00:01:29,481
kernel of the table, with all
the arithmetic done mod two.

24
00:01:30,067 --> 00:01:33,961
Now if one bit flips, the rows that
contain it start saying odd, and the

25
00:01:33,961 --> 00:01:38,513
pattern of odd rows you get is the column
of the table at the position that flipped.

26
00:01:38,933 --> 00:01:41,757
<font color="#7FD4E6">Oh, that's the address
trick from Steane's code.</font>

27
00:01:42,100 --> 00:01:46,078
Right. Now you can see where Steane's
code got it from, and nothing quantum

28
00:01:46,078 --> 00:01:50,002
happens in this picture at all, it's
an ordinary parity check, the kind of

29
00:01:50,002 --> 00:01:52,743
check the memory in your computer
runs all day long.

30
00:01:54,267 --> 00:01:57,859
So now you take two tables with
the same number of columns,

31
00:01:57,859 --> 00:02:01,575
which we'll call H X and H Z,
and turn every row of the first

32
00:02:01,575 --> 00:02:05,167
into an X check and every row
of the second into a Z check.

33
00:02:05,767 --> 00:02:09,192
Two X checks always commute,
and so do two Z checks, since

34
00:02:09,192 --> 00:02:12,557
a letter never clashes with
itself, which leaves just one

35
00:02:12,558 --> 00:02:15,983
kind of pair to worry about,
an X check against a Z check.

36
00:02:16,567 --> 00:02:19,882
And as we saw last time, an X
and a Z clash on every qubit

37
00:02:19,882 --> 00:02:23,488
they share, so the pair commutes
when they share an even number

38
00:02:23,487 --> 00:02:26,395
of qubits and anticommutes when
that number is odd.

39
00:02:26,967 --> 00:02:30,981
Let me try the tempting recipe on
the smallest table we know, the two

40
00:02:30,981 --> 00:02:34,641
rows of the bit flip code, written
once with X and once with Z.

41
00:02:35,833 --> 00:02:41,621
<font color="#7FD4E6">X X I and I Z Z only share
qubit two. So they anticommute!</font>

42
00:02:41,967 --> 00:02:45,766
That's it. Qubit two is the
single clash, so a perfectly good

43
00:02:45,766 --> 00:02:49,628
classical code still breaks the
recipe, because one row of one

44
00:02:49,629 --> 00:02:53,301
table overlaps one row of the
other an odd number of times.

45
00:02:53,900 --> 00:02:59,240
So the missing half is this. Every row
of H X has to share an even number of

46
00:02:59,240 --> 00:03:04,438
ones with every row of H Z, and if you
collect all those overlaps into one

47
00:03:04,438 --> 00:03:09,636
product, the condition reads H X times
H Z transpose equals zero, mod two.

48
00:03:10,233 --> 00:03:14,112
When that holds, the checks define
a code, and the number of logical

49
00:03:14,112 --> 00:03:17,644
qubits is n minus the number of
independent X checks minus the

50
00:03:17,644 --> 00:03:21,350
number of independent Z checks,
the same halving count as before.

51
00:03:22,900 --> 00:03:27,134
Once the checks commute, the two kinds
of error stay out of each other's way.

52
00:03:27,134 --> 00:03:31,201
A bit flip only trips the Z checks,
and the pattern it leaves is H Z times

53
00:03:31,201 --> 00:03:35,101
the error, so you can decode that half
like an ordinary classical code.

54
00:03:35,700 --> 00:03:39,297
A phase flip trips nothing but
the X checks, leaving H X times

55
00:03:39,297 --> 00:03:42,953
its pattern, and that half gets
a classical decoder of its own.

56
00:03:43,400 --> 00:03:47,190
<font color="#7FD4E6">And a Y error, which is a bit
flip and a phase flip at once?</font>

57
00:03:48,267 --> 00:03:51,700
It lights up both halves at the
same time, and each decoder fixes

58
00:03:51,700 --> 00:03:54,973
its own part of it, one undoing
the bit flip and the other the

59
00:03:54,973 --> 00:03:57,870
phase flip, without ever needing
to talk to each other.

60
00:03:58,467 --> 00:04:01,621
I find that the nicest part of
the construction, because all

61
00:04:01,621 --> 00:04:04,507
the hard work of decoding a
quantum code turns into two

62
00:04:04,507 --> 00:04:07,500
classical problems that people
already knew how to solve.

63
00:04:09,033 --> 00:04:12,922
So let's go back to Steane's code,
where both tables are the same H,

64
00:04:12,922 --> 00:04:16,578
and the condition becomes H times
its own transpose equals zero.

65
00:04:17,167 --> 00:04:22,150
If you check, any two rows of the Hamming
table share two ones, and each row shares

66
00:04:22,150 --> 00:04:26,222
four with itself, so every overlap is
even, and the condition holds.

67
00:04:26,667 --> 00:04:30,533
<font color="#7FD4E6">So the bit flip table failed,
but the Hamming table passes.</font>

68
00:04:30,867 --> 00:04:34,294
Right. Classical coding theorists
have a name for a table like

69
00:04:34,294 --> 00:04:37,890
that, they say the code contains
its dual, and it's the one extra

70
00:04:37,890 --> 00:04:41,429
demand that a quantum code makes
on top of the classical theory.

71
00:04:42,967 --> 00:04:47,827
So two classical codes laid over the
same qubits, one for each kind of error,

72
00:04:47,827 --> 00:04:52,815
give you a quantum code as long as their
tables overlap evenly, and codes built

73
00:04:52,815 --> 00:04:56,907
this way are called CSS codes, after
Calderbank, Shor and Steane.

74
00:04:57,333 --> 00:05:01,552
<font color="#7FD4E6">But these checks still reach right
across the whole code. Do they have to?</font>

75
00:05:01,900 --> 00:05:06,448
They don't, and in section eight point
five we'll meet the surface code, a CSS

76
00:05:06,448 --> 00:05:10,759
code whose checks only ever touch four
neighbouring qubits on a flat grid.
